{
  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Graphics {#sec-chapter6}\n",
        "\n",
        "## Introduction \n",
        "In this chapter, we introduce two modules for graphics: `matplotlib` and `seaborn`. Matplotlib offers two primary approaches for creating figures: (i) the MATLAB-style (or pyplot) approach, and (ii) the object-oriented programming (OOP) approach. The MATLAB-style tools are contained in the `pyplot` (`plt`) interface. For plot styles and color defaults, we rely on the `seaborn` module."
      ],
      "id": "88d9f729"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
        "import seaborn as sns\n",
        "import scipy.stats as stats"
      ],
      "id": "2b65ed86",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Setting plotting styles\n",
        "The theme of a figure refers to the overall appearance, including colors, fonts, and line styles. We use the `seaborn` module to set the theme of our figures. The are five preset seaborn themes: `darkgrid`, `whitegrid`, `dark`, `white`, and `ticks`. The `sns.set_style` function can be used to set these themes. The default theme is `darkgrid`. For more information, see [Controlling figure aesthetics](https://seaborn.pydata.org/tutorial/aesthetics.html) from the documentation."
      ],
      "id": "4fb1b42e"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Setting seaborn style\n",
        "#sns.set_style('whitegrid')\n",
        "#sns.set_style('dark')\n",
        "#sns.set_style('white')\n",
        "#sns.set_style('ticks')\n",
        "sns.set_style('darkgrid') "
      ],
      "id": "23ffcfc6",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The `plt.style.available` function lists all available styles in Matplotlib. "
      ],
      "id": "0a24127f"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# List available styles\n",
        "plt.style.available"
      ],
      "id": "13b8111d",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Alternatively, we can set the `darkgrid` style using the `plt.style` function. In the following example (@fig-fig6.1), we first create a figure using `plt.figure(figsize=(5, 5))`, which sets the size of the figure area to 5x5 inches. In Matplotlib, plots are contained within a `Figure`. We then set the plotting style with `plt.style.use('seaborn-v0_8-darkgrid')` and display the plot using `plt.plot()`."
      ],
      "id": "a7c23b9f"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| fig-cap: The `seaborn-v0_8-darkgrid` style\n",
        "#| label: fig-fig6.1\n",
        "plt.figure(figsize=(5,4)) # set figure size to 5x5 inches\n",
        "plt.style.use('seaborn-v0_8-darkgrid')\n",
        "plt.plot()\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fig-fig61",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## MATLAB Style Approach\n",
        "\n",
        "### Line plots\n",
        "In the MATLAB-style approach, we use the `plt.plot` function to create line plots. The `plt.plot` function can be used to create a wide variety of line plots, including simple line plots, line plots with markers, and line plots with different styles. The `plt.plot` function can also be used to add multiple lines to a single figure. The following code chunk generates the line plots shown in @fig-fig6.2. "
      ],
      "id": "d7761dc3"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Data\n",
        "np.random.seed(12345)\n",
        "y =np.random.randn(20)\n",
        "x = np.cumsum(np.random.rand(20))"
      ],
      "id": "d18f60e0",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: fig-fig6.2\n",
        "#| fig-cap: Line Plots\n",
        "#| layout-ncol: 2\n",
        "#| fig-subcap:\n",
        "#|   - Line Plot 1\n",
        "#|   - Line Plot 2\n",
        "#|   - Line Plot 3\n",
        "#|   - Line Plot 4\n",
        "# Line Plot 1\n",
        "plt.figure(figsize=(4,4))\n",
        "plt.plot(y)\n",
        "plt.show()\n",
        "# Line Plot 2\n",
        "plt.figure(figsize=(4,4))\n",
        "plt.plot(y,'r-o',label='Line Plot 2')\n",
        "plt.legend()\n",
        "plt.xlabel('x label')\n",
        "plt.title('Line Plot 2')\n",
        "plt.ylabel('y label')\n",
        "plt.show()\n",
        "# Line Plot 3\n",
        "plt.figure(figsize=(4,4))\n",
        "plt.plot(x,y,'b-d',label='Line Plot 3')\n",
        "plt.legend()\n",
        "plt.xlabel('x label')\n",
        "plt.title('Line Plot 3')\n",
        "plt.ylabel('y label')\n",
        "plt.show()\n",
        "# Line Plot 4\n",
        "plt.figure(figsize=(4,4))\n",
        "plt.plot(x,y, linestyle = \"-\", color='steelblue' , label='Line Plot 4')\n",
        "plt.legend()\n",
        "plt.xlabel('x label')\n",
        "plt.title('Line Plot 4')\n",
        "plt.ylabel('y label')\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fig-fig62",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Some options used to customize the appearance of axes and lines are described in the following list:\n",
        "\n",
        "* In @fig-fig6.2-2, `r-o` indicates the color red (r), a solid line (-), and a circle (o) marker.\n",
        "* In @fig-fig6.2-3, `b-d` indicates the color blue (b), a solid line (-), and a diamond (d) marker.\n",
        "* In @fig-fig6.2-4, the line style and color are specified using `linestyle = \"-\"` and `color='steelblue'`.\n",
        "* Titles are added with `plt.title()`, and legends are added using `plt.legend()`. The legend requires that the line includes a label.\n",
        "* Labels for the x- and y-axes are added using `plt.xlabel()` and `plt.ylabel()`.\n",
        "\n",
        "In the following example, we specify the arguments of the `plt.plot` function explicitly."
      ],
      "id": "a8ee4a11"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: fig-fig6.3\n",
        "#| fig-cap: Specifying arguments explicitly\n",
        "# Specify arguments\n",
        "plt.figure(figsize=(5,4))\n",
        "plt.plot(x, y,\n",
        "    alpha = 1,\n",
        "    color = \"#FF7F00\",\n",
        "    label = \"Line Label\",\n",
        "    linestyle = \"-\",\n",
        "    linewidth = 2,\n",
        "    marker = \"o\",\n",
        "    markeredgecolor = \"#000000\",\n",
        "    markeredgewidth = 1,\n",
        "    markerfacecolor = \"#FF7F99\",\n",
        "    markersize = 5,\n",
        ")\n",
        "plt.legend()\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"x label\")\n",
        "plt.title(\"A line plot\")\n",
        "plt.ylabel(\"y label\")\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fig-fig63",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The most useful arguments of the `plt.plot` function are listed below:\n",
        "\n",
        "* `alpha`: Alpha (transparency) of the plot- default is 1 (no transparency)\n",
        "* `color`: Color description for the line. It can be specified in several ways, including:\n",
        "  - Color name (e.g., `'red'`, `'steelblue'`, `'green'`)\n",
        "  - Short color code (e.g., `'r'`, `'g'`, `'b'`)\n",
        "  - Grayscale value (e.g., `'0.5'` for gray)\n",
        "  - Hexadecimal code (e.g., `'#FF0000'` for red)\n",
        "  - RGB tuple (e.g., `(1.0, 0.0, 0.0)` for red)\n",
        "  - HTML color names (e.g., `'DarkMagenta'`)\n",
        "* `label`: A label for the line, used in the legend\n",
        "* `linestyle`: A line style symbol\n",
        "* `linewidth`: A positive integer indicating the width of the line\n",
        "* `marker`: A marker shape symbol or character\n",
        "* `markeredgecolor`: Color of the edge (a line) around the marker\n",
        "* `markeredgewidth`: Width of the edge (a line) around the marker\n",
        "* `markerfacecolor`: Face color of the marker\n",
        "* `markersize`: A positive integer indicating the size of the marker\n",
        "\n",
        "Some commonly used options for `color`,  `linestyle` and  `marker` are shown in @tbl-t6.1. For more information, see [Colors](https://matplotlib.org/stable/users/explain/colors/index.html#tutorials-colors), [Linestyles](https://matplotlib.org/stable/gallery/lines_bars_and_markers/linestyles.html) and [Marker reference](https://matplotlib.org/stable/gallery/lines_bars_and_markers/marker_reference.html) from the documentation.\n",
        "\n",
        "| **color** | **linestyle** | **marker** |\n",
        "|:-|:-|:-|\n",
        "| blue: `b`  | Solid: `-` | Point: `.` |\n",
        "| green: `g` | dashed: `--` | pixel: `,` |\n",
        "| red: `r`  | dashdot: `-.`| circle: `o`|\n",
        "| cyan: `c`| dotted: `:`| square: `s` |\n",
        "| magenta: `m` |  | diamond: `D` |\n",
        "| yellow: `y`  || thin diamond: `d` |\n",
        "| black: `k` | | cross: `x`  |\n",
        "| white: `w` |   | plus: `+` |\n",
        "| || star: `*`|\n",
        "|| | pentagon: p |\n",
        "||  | triangles: `^,v,<,>` |\n",
        "\n",
        ": Commonly used colors, line styles and marker {#tbl-t6.1}\n",
        "\n",
        "The following code chunk, which generates @fig-fig6.4, shows alternative ways to specify `color`."
      ],
      "id": "9a64599b"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {
        "fig-height": 2
      },
      "source": [
        "#| label: fig-fig6.4\n",
        "#| fig-cap: Specifying the `color` options\n",
        "# Alternative way to specify color\n",
        "x = np.linspace(0, 2, 500)\n",
        "plt.figure(figsize=(5,4))\n",
        "plt.plot(x, np.exp(x + 0.2), color='steelblue') # specify color by name\n",
        "plt.plot(x, np.exp(x - 0.1), color='g') # short color code (rgbcmyk)\n",
        "plt.plot(x, np.exp(x - 0.4), color='0.2') # Grayscale between 0 and 1\n",
        "plt.plot(x, np.exp(x - 0.6), color='#04fa25') # Hex code (RRGGBB from 00 to FF)\n",
        "plt.plot(x, np.exp(x - 0.8), color=(1.0,0.2,0.3)) # RGB tuple, values 0 and 1\n",
        "plt.plot(x, np.exp(x - 1), color='DarkMagenta'); # all HTML color names supported\n",
        "plt.xlabel('x')\n",
        "plt.ylabel('Exponential function')\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fig-fig64",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "In @fig-fig6.4, we have a single figure with multiple line plots. Thus, to add multiple lines to a single figure, we need to call the `plt.plot` function multiple times. \n",
        "\n",
        "The following code chunk, which generates @fig-fig6.5, provide alternative ways for specifying the `linestyle` options. "
      ],
      "id": "7dae284c"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {
        "fig-height": 1
      },
      "source": [
        "#| label: fig-fig6.5\n",
        "#| fig-cap: Specifying the `linestyle` options\n",
        "# Alternative way to specify linestyle\n",
        "x = np.linspace(0, 5, 500)\n",
        "plt.figure(figsize=(5,4))\n",
        "plt.plot(x, np.log(x+0.1), linestyle='solid',linewidth=2)\n",
        "plt.plot(x, np.log(x + 0.6), linestyle='dashed',linewidth=2)\n",
        "plt.plot(x, np.log(x + 1), linestyle='dashdot',linewidth=2)\n",
        "plt.plot(x, np.log(x + 1.5), linestyle='dotted',linewidth=2)\n",
        "plt.axhline(0, color=\"k\", linestyle=\"-\",linewidth=1, alpha=0.5)\n",
        "plt.xlabel('x')\n",
        "plt.ylabel('Natural logarithm')\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fig-fig65",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        " The `plt.setp` function can be used to set options for plot elements, as shown in the following example (@fig-fig6.6):"
      ],
      "id": "6b500b30"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: fig-fig6.6\n",
        "#| fig-cap: Using the `plt.setp` function\n",
        "np.random.seed(45)\n",
        "x = np.random.randn(10)\n",
        "plt.figure(figsize=(5,4))\n",
        "h = plt.plot(x)\n",
        "plt.setp(h,\n",
        "    alpha=1,\n",
        "    linestyle=\"--\",\n",
        "    linewidth=1.5,\n",
        "    label=\"Line Label\",\n",
        "    marker=\"d\",\n",
        "    color=\"red\",\n",
        "    markeredgecolor=\"black\",\n",
        "    markerfacecolor=\"green\",\n",
        "    markersize=5\n",
        ")\n",
        "plt.axhline(0, color=\"k\", linestyle=\"-\",linewidth=1, alpha=0.5)\n",
        "plt.legend()\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"x label\")\n",
        "plt.ylabel(\"y label\")\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fig-fig66",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The `plt.getp` function retrieves the list of properties for a Matplotlib object:"
      ],
      "id": "93991efb"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "plt.getp(h)"
      ],
      "id": "aac05bb7",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The `plt.xlim` and `plt.ylim` can be used to set axes limits, as illustrated in the following examples. "
      ],
      "id": "957c55e3"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: fig-fig6.7\n",
        "#| fig-cap: Using the `plt.xlim` and `plt.ylim` functions\n",
        "x = np.linspace(0, 10, 1000)\n",
        "plt.figure(figsize=(5,4))\n",
        "plt.plot(x, np.cos(x))\n",
        "plt.axhline(0, color=\"k\", linestyle=\"-\",linewidth=1, alpha=0.5)\n",
        "plt.xlim(-1, 12)\n",
        "plt.ylim(-1.5, 1.5)\n",
        "plt.xlabel('x')\n",
        "plt.ylabel('Cosine function')\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fig-fig67",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: fig-fig6.8\n",
        "#| fig-cap: Using the `plt.axis` function\n",
        "#  Setting x and y limits with plt.axis()\n",
        "plt.figure(figsize=(5,4))\n",
        "plt.plot(x, np.cos(x))\n",
        "plt.axis([-1, 12, -1.5, 1.5]) # specify [xmin,xmax,ymin,ymax]\n",
        "plt.xlabel('x')\n",
        "plt.ylabel('Cosine function')\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fig-fig68",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Scatter plots\n",
        "Scatter plots can be generated using the `plt.scatter` function or the `plt.plot` function with the option `linestyle=\"\"`. Consider the following example, which generates @fig-fig6.9."
      ],
      "id": "31fa5e83"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: fig-fig6.9\n",
        "#| fig-cap: Scatter plot using `plt.scatter`\n",
        "# Scatter plots using plt.scatter\n",
        "np.random.seed(45)\n",
        "z = np.random.randn(100, 2)\n",
        "z[:, 1] = 0.5 * z[:, 0] + np.sqrt(0.5) * z[:, 1]\n",
        "x = z[:, 0]\n",
        "y = z[:, 1]\n",
        "plt.figure(figsize=(5,4))\n",
        "plt.scatter(x, y, c=\"steelblue\", marker=\"o\", alpha=1, s = 25, label=\"Scatter Data\")\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"x\", fontsize=12)\n",
        "plt.ylabel(\"y\", fontsize=12)\n",
        "plt.title(\"A scatter plot\", fontsize=14)\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fig-fig69",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Alternatively, we can use the `plt.plot` function with the option `linestyle=\"\"` as shown in the following example. "
      ],
      "id": "acb4a478"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: fig-fig6.10\n",
        "#| fig-cap: Scatter plot using `plt.plot`\n",
        "# Scatter plot using plt.plot\n",
        "plt.figure(figsize=(5,4))\n",
        "plt.plot(x, y, linestyle=\"\", c=\"steelblue\", marker=\"o\", ms=5, \n",
        "    alpha=1, label=\"Scatter Data\")\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"x\", fontsize=12)\n",
        "plt.ylabel(\"y\", fontsize=12)\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fig-fig610",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Bar plots\n",
        "The `plt.bar` function is used to create bar plots. The following code chunk generates the bar plots shown in @fig-fig6.11 and @fig-fig6.12."
      ],
      "id": "4afbc77d"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: fig-fig6.11\n",
        "#| fig-cap: Bar plot\n",
        "np.random.seed(123)\n",
        "y = np.random.rand(5)\n",
        "x = np.arange(5)\n",
        "plt.figure(figsize=(5,4))\n",
        "b=plt.bar(x,y, width = 1, color = 'steelblue', \n",
        "    edgecolor = 'black', linewidth = 0.5)\n",
        "plt.title(\"Bar Plot\")\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"x label\")\n",
        "plt.ylabel(\"y label\")\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fig-fig611",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: fig-fig6.12\n",
        "#| fig-cap: Bar plot\n",
        "np.random.seed(45)\n",
        "y = np.random.rand(5)\n",
        "x = [\"G1\", \"G2\",\"G3\",\"G4\",\"G5\"]\n",
        "plt.figure(figsize=(5,4))\n",
        "b=plt.bar(x,y, width = 1, color = 'steelblue', \n",
        "    edgecolor = 'black', linewidth = 0.5)\n",
        "plt.title(\"Bar Plot\")\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fig-fig612",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "In the following example, we generate a horizontal bar plot using the `plt.barh` function."
      ],
      "id": "43184fe7"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: fig-fig6.12-1\n",
        "#| fig-cap: Horizontal Bar plot\n",
        "# Horizontal Bar plot\n",
        "np.random.seed(45)\n",
        "y = np.random.rand(5)\n",
        "x = [\"G1\", \"G2\",\"G3\",\"G4\",\"G5\"]\n",
        "plt.figure(figsize=(5,4))\n",
        "b=plt.barh(x,y, height = 0.5, color = 'steelblue', \n",
        "    edgecolor = 'black', linewidth = 0.5)\n",
        "plt.title(\"Horizontal Bar Plot\")\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fig-fig612-1",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Pie plots\n",
        "The `plt.pie` function is used to create pie plots. The following code chunk generates the pie plot shown in @fig-fig6.13."
      ],
      "id": "85b46462"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: fig-fig6.13\n",
        "#| fig-cap: Pie plot\n",
        "# Pie  plots\n",
        "np.random.seed(45)\n",
        "y = np.random.rand(5)\n",
        "y = y/sum(y)\n",
        "y[y<.05] = .05\n",
        "labels=['One','Two','Three','Four','Five']\n",
        "colors = ['#FF00FF','#FFF000','#00FF0F','#00FFF0','#00000F']\n",
        "explode=np.array([0.1,0,0,0.1,0])\n",
        "plt.figure(figsize=(5,5))\n",
        "plt.pie(y,labels=labels,colors=colors,shadow=True,explode=explode)\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fig-fig613",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Histograms\n",
        "We can use the `plt.hist` function to generate histograms. In the following example, the `histtype` option assumes the following options: `bar`, `barstacked`, `step`, and `stepfilled`."
      ],
      "id": "c0379fb8"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: fig-fig6.14\n",
        "#| fig-cap: Histograms\n",
        "#  Histograms\n",
        "np.random.seed(45)\n",
        "x = np.random.randn(1000)\n",
        "plt.figure(figsize=(5,4))\n",
        "plt.hist(x, bins = 30, density=True, color='steelblue',\n",
        "         histtype=\"bar\",edgecolor='black',linewidth=0.5)\n",
        "plt.xlabel('x')\n",
        "plt.ylabel('Density')\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fig-fig614",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "In the following example, we show how to add multiple plots to the same axes. The first plot is a histogram, and the second plot is the probability density function (PDF) of the normal distribution."
      ],
      "id": "3d3fa45b"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: fig-fig6.16\n",
        "#| fig-cap: Multiple plots on the same axes\n",
        "# Multiple Plots on the Same Axes\n",
        "np.random.seed(45)\n",
        "x=stats.norm.rvs(loc=2,scale=1.5,size=1000)\n",
        "plt.figure(figsize=(5,4))\n",
        "plt.hist(x, bins = 30,density=True,label = 'Empirical')\n",
        "pdfx = np.linspace(x.min(),x.max(),1000)\n",
        "pdfy = stats.norm.pdf(pdfx,loc=2,scale=1.5)\n",
        "plt.plot(pdfx,pdfy,'r-',label = 'PDF')\n",
        "plt.xlabel('x')\n",
        "plt.ylabel('Density')\n",
        "plt.legend()\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fig-fig616",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Kernel Density and Empirical CDF plots\n",
        "The `sns.kdeplot` function from the `seaborn` module can be used to create a kernel density plot. The kernel density estimate is a non-parametric way to estimate the probability density function of a random variable. In the following example, we generate random draws from a t-distribution and a normal distribution, and then plot their kernel density estimates. The `fill=True` option fills the area under the curve, and the `alpha` option sets the transparency of the fill color."
      ],
      "id": "db6d8d47"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: fig-fig6.16-1\n",
        "#| fig-cap: Kernel density plots\n",
        "# Kernel Density Plot\n",
        "np.random.seed(45)\n",
        "x = stats.t.rvs(df=3, loc=0, scale=1, size=1000)\n",
        "y = np.random.randn(1000)\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.figure(figsize=(5, 4))\n",
        "sns.kdeplot(x, color='steelblue', fill=True, alpha=0.5, linewidth=1, label='t-distribution')\n",
        "sns.kdeplot(y, color='orange', fill=True, alpha=0.5, linewidth=1, label='Normal distribution')\n",
        "plt.xlabel('x')\n",
        "plt.xlim(-6, 6)\n",
        "plt.ylabel('Density')\n",
        "plt.title('Kernel Density Plots')\n",
        "plt.legend()\n",
        "plt.tight_layout()\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fig-fig616-1",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The empirical cumulative distribution function (CDF) can be plotted using the `sns.ecdfplot` function from the `seaborn` module. The empirical CDF is a step function that estimates the cumulative distribution function of a random variable based on a sample. In the following example, we generate random draws from a t-distribution and a normal distribution, and then plot their empirical CDFs."
      ],
      "id": "37bb315c"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: fig-fig6.16-2\n",
        "#| fig-cap: Empirical CDF plots\n",
        "# Empirical CDF plot\n",
        "np.random.seed(45)\n",
        "x = np.random.randn(1000)\n",
        "y = stats.t.rvs(df=3, loc=0, scale=1, size=1000)\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.figure(figsize=(5, 4))\n",
        "sns.ecdfplot(x=x, color='steelblue', linewidth=1, label='Normal distribution')\n",
        "sns.ecdfplot(x=y, color='orange', linewidth=1, label='t-distribution')\n",
        "plt.xlabel('x', fontsize=12)\n",
        "plt.xlim(-6, 6)\n",
        "plt.ylim(0, 1.05)\n",
        "plt.ylabel('Cumulative Probability', fontsize=12)\n",
        "plt.title('Empirical CDF', fontsize=14)\n",
        "plt.legend()\n",
        "plt.tight_layout()\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fig-fig616-2",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Multiple plots on the same figure\n",
        "To add subplots to a figure, we can use the `plt.subplot` function. In the example below, we use `plt.subplot(2, 2, p)` to divide the figure area into a 2x2 grid, where p indicates the position of the subplot within the grid. "
      ],
      "id": "7f0d8304"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: fig-fig6.15\n",
        "#| fig-cap: Using the `plt.subplot` function\n",
        "plt.figure(figsize=(7,7))\n",
        "np.random.seed(45)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Panel 1\n",
        "plt.subplot(2,2,1)\n",
        "y1 = np.random.randn(100)\n",
        "plt.plot(y1)\n",
        "plt.title('Plot 1')\n",
        "\n",
        "# Panel 2\n",
        "plt.subplot(2,2,2)\n",
        "y2 = np.random.rand(5)\n",
        "x2 = np.arange(5)\n",
        "plt.bar(x2, y2, label='Bar Chart', color='steelblue', edgecolor='black', linewidth=0.5)\n",
        "plt.legend()\n",
        "plt.xlabel('x label')\n",
        "plt.ylabel('y label')\n",
        "plt.title('Plot 2')\n",
        "\n",
        "# Panel 3\n",
        "plt.subplot(2,2,3)\n",
        "y3 = np.random.rand(5)\n",
        "y3 = y3 / y3.sum()\n",
        "y3[y3 < 0.05] = 0.05\n",
        "labels = ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D', 'E']\n",
        "colors = ['#FF00FF','#FFF000','#00FF0F','#00FFF0','#F0000F']\n",
        "plt.pie(y3, labels=labels, colors=colors, autopct='%1.1f%%', startangle=90)\n",
        "plt.title('Plot 3')\n",
        "\n",
        "# Panel 4\n",
        "plt.subplot(2,2,4)\n",
        "z = np.random.randn(100,2)\n",
        "z[:,1] = 0.5 * z[:,0] + np.sqrt(0.5) * z[:,1]\n",
        "x4 = z[:,0]\n",
        "y4 = z[:,1]\n",
        "plt.scatter(x4, y4, s=20, color='steelblue', alpha=0.8, edgecolor='black')\n",
        "plt.title('Plot 4')\n",
        "plt.xlabel('x values')\n",
        "plt.ylabel('y values')\n",
        "\n",
        "# Padding\n",
        "plt.subplots_adjust(hspace=0.5, wspace=0.5)\n",
        "plt.tight_layout()\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fig-fig615",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The padding between subplots can be adjusted using the `plt.subplots_adjust` function. In @fig-fig6.15, we use `plt.subplots_adjust(hspace=0.5, wspace=0.5)` to add horizontal and vertical spacing between subplots.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Saving figures\n",
        "The `plt.savefig` function allows user to save figures in a wide variety of formats. The supported formats can be listed using the `canvas.get_supported_filetypes` method of the figure object, as shown below:"
      ],
      "id": "b44c13e7"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# The supported file types\n",
        "fig = plt.figure()\n",
        "fig.canvas.get_supported_filetypes()"
      ],
      "id": "ac3f3ac7",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "In the following example, we show how to save a figure in PDF, PNG, and SVG formats. The `dpi` option can be used to specify the resolution of the image in dots per inch (DPI). A higher DPI value results in a higher resolution image. The default DPI value is 100."
      ],
      "id": "d4160daf"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: fig-fig6.17\n",
        "#| fig-cap: Saving plots\n",
        "# Exporting plots\n",
        "np.random.seed(45)\n",
        "plt.figure(figsize=(5, 4))\n",
        "plt.plot(np.random.randn(10,2))\n",
        "# plt.savefig('figures/Myfigure.pdf') # PDF export\n",
        "# plt.savefig('figures/Myfigure.png') # PNG export\n",
        "# plt.savefig('figures/Myfigure.svg') # Scalable Vector Graphics export\n",
        "# plt.savefig('figures/Myfigure2.png', dpi = 600) # High resolution PNG export\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fig-fig617",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## OOP Approach\n",
        "The object-oriented interface in Matplotlib offers greater control over figures. In this approach, plotting functions are methods associated with explicit Figure and Axes objects. To create a plot, we begin by initializing a Figure object and an Axes object. The Figure object, referred to as `fig`, can be created as follows:"
      ],
      "id": "f0dfac55"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Create fig object\n",
        "fig = plt.figure(figsize=(5, 4))\n",
        "type(fig)"
      ],
      "id": "20eb9160",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The `fig` object (an instance of the `plt.figure.Figure` class) serves as a container that holds all components, including axes, graphics, text, and labels. The Axes object, referred to as `ax`, can be created as follows:"
      ],
      "id": "eb9e2201"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Create ax object\n",
        "fig = plt.figure(figsize=(5, 4))\n",
        "ax = plt.axes()\n",
        "type(ax)"
      ],
      "id": "32e04757",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The `ax` (an instance of the `plt.axes._axes.Axes` class) object represents the box that contains the plot elements making up the visualization.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Line plots\n",
        "In the OOP approach, we can create line plots using the `ax.plot` function. The `ax.plot` function is similar to the `plt.plot` function, but it is called on the `ax` object instead of the `plt` module. In the following example, we generate a line plot of the sine function using the `ax.plot` function. The `ax.axhline` function is used to add a horizontal line at $y=0$."
      ],
      "id": "57a4a9ca"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: fig-fig6.18\n",
        "#| fig-cap: Using the `ax.plot` function\n",
        "fig = plt.figure(figsize=(5, 3))\n",
        "ax = plt.axes()\n",
        "x = np.linspace(0, 10, 1000)\n",
        "ax.plot(x,np.sin(x),color='r')\n",
        "# Add a horizontal line at y=0\n",
        "ax.axhline(0, color='k', linestyle='-', linewidth=1, alpha=0.5)\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fig-fig618",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Note that in @fig-fig6.18, the `ax.plot` function serves the same purpose as the `plt.plot` function. While most `plt` functions translate directly to `ax` methods (such as `plt.plot` $\\rightarrow$ `ax.plot`, and `plt.legend` $\\rightarrow$ `ax.legend`), this is not the case for all commands. Below, we give some commonly used translations.\n",
        "\n",
        "* `plt.plot()`  $\\rightarrow$ `ax.plot()`\n",
        "* `plt.legend()`  $\\rightarrow$ `ax.legend()`\n",
        "* `plt.xlabel()`  $\\rightarrow$ `ax.set_xlabel()`\n",
        "* `plt.ylabel()`  $\\rightarrow$ `ax.set_ylabel()`\n",
        "* `plt.xlim()`  $\\rightarrow$ `ax.set_xlim()`\n",
        "* `plt.ylim()`  $\\rightarrow$ `ax.set_ylim()`\n",
        "* `plt.title()`  $\\rightarrow$ `ax.set_title`\n",
        "* `plt.subplot()`  $\\rightarrow$ `ax.fig.add_subplot()`\n",
        "* `plt.hline()`  $\\rightarrow$ `ax.axhline()`\n",
        "* `plt.vline()`  $\\rightarrow$ `ax.axvline()`\n",
        "* `plt.text()`  $\\rightarrow$ `ax.text()`\n",
        "* `plt.annotate()`  $\\rightarrow$ `ax.annotate()`\n",
        "* `plt.grid()`  $\\rightarrow$ `ax.grid()`\n",
        "* `plt.subplots()`  $\\rightarrow$ `fig, ax = plt.subplots()`\n",
        "* `plt.show()`  $\\rightarrow$ `plt.show()` (remains unchanged) \n",
        "* `plt.savefig()`  $\\rightarrow$ `fig.savefig()`\n",
        "\n",
        "In the following example, we generate a line plot of the sine function using the `ax.plot` function. The `ax.set_title`, `ax.set_xlabel`, and `ax.set_ylabel` functions are used to set the title and labels of the axes. The `ax.set_xlim` and `ax.set_ylim` functions are used to set the limits of the x- and y-axes, respectively. Finally, we add a legend using the `ax.legend` function."
      ],
      "id": "d8d4e371"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: fig-fig6.19\n",
        "#| fig-cap: Using the `ax.plot` function\n",
        "x = np.linspace(0, 10, 1000)\n",
        "fig = plt.figure(figsize=(5, 3))\n",
        "ax = plt.axes()\n",
        "ax.plot(x, np.sin(x), color='steelblue', label='sin(x)')\n",
        "ax.axhline(0, color='k', linestyle='-', linewidth=1, alpha=0.5)\n",
        "ax.set_title('The sine function')\n",
        "ax.set_xlabel('x')\n",
        "ax.set_ylabel('sin(x)')\n",
        "ax.set_xlim(0,10)\n",
        "ax.set_ylim(-2,2)\n",
        "ax.legend()\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fig-fig619",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "In the OOP approach, rather than calling `ax` methods individually,\n",
        "it is often more convenient to use the `ax.set()` function as shown in the next examples. In this example `r'$\\sin(x)$'` and `r'$\\cos(x)$'` are used to render the sine and cosine functions in LaTeX format. The `r` before the string indicates that it is a raw string, which allows us to use LaTeX commands without escaping them."
      ],
      "id": "6f4a1175"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: fig-fig6.20\n",
        "#| fig-cap: Using the `ax.set` function\n",
        "x = np.linspace(0, 10, 1000)\n",
        "fig = plt.figure(figsize=(5, 3))\n",
        "ax = plt.axes()\n",
        "ax.plot(x, np.sin(x), color='steelblue', label=r'$\\sin(x)$', linewidth=1)\n",
        "ax.plot(x, np.cos(x), color='orange', label=r'$\\cos(x)$', linewidth=1)\n",
        "ax.axhline(0, color='k', linestyle='-', linewidth=1, alpha=0.5)\n",
        "ax.set(\n",
        "    xlim=(0, 10),\n",
        "    ylim=(-1.2, 1.2),\n",
        "    xlabel='x',\n",
        "    ylabel=r\"$\\sin(x)$ and $\\cos(x)$\",\n",
        "    title=\"Sine and cosine functions\"\n",
        ")\n",
        "ax.legend(loc='upper right', frameon=False)\n",
        "fig.tight_layout()\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fig-fig620",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "In the above examples, we create the `fig` and `ax` objects separately using `fig = plt.figure()` and `ax = plt.axes()`. Alternatively, we can generate both objects with a single command by using the `plt.subplots` function. Consider the following example:"
      ],
      "id": "8416aa09"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: fig-fig6.21\n",
        "#| fig-cap: Using the `plt.subplots` function\n",
        "# Improved version: clearer labels, LaTeX formatting, grid, and tight layout\n",
        "x = np.linspace(0, 10, 1000)\n",
        "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(5, 3))\n",
        "ax.plot(x, np.sin(x), label=r'$\\sin(x)$', color='steelblue', linewidth=1)\n",
        "ax.plot(x, np.cos(x), label=r'$\\cos(x)$', color='orange', linewidth=1)\n",
        "ax.axhline(0, color='k', linestyle='-', linewidth=1, alpha=0.5)\n",
        "ax.set(\n",
        "    xlim=(0, 10),\n",
        "    ylim=(-1.2, 1.2),\n",
        "    xlabel='x',\n",
        "    ylabel='Value',\n",
        "    title='Sine and Cosine Functions'\n",
        ")\n",
        "ax.legend(loc='upper right', frameon=True)\n",
        "fig.tight_layout()\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fig-fig621",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Scatter plots\n",
        "Scatter plots can be generated using the `ax.scatter` function or the `ax.plot` function with the option `linestyle=\"\"`. The following example generates a scatter plot using the `ax.scatter` function."
      ],
      "id": "d15073cd"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: fig-fig6.22\n",
        "#| fig-cap: Scatter plot using `ax.scatter`\n",
        "# Scatter plot using ax.scatter\n",
        "np.random.seed(45)\n",
        "fig = plt.figure(figsize=(5, 4))\n",
        "ax = plt.axes()\n",
        "z =np.random.randn(100,2)\n",
        "z[:,1] = 0.5*z[:,0] + np.sqrt(0.5)*z[:,1]\n",
        "x=z[:,0]\n",
        "y=z[:,1]\n",
        "ax.scatter(x,y,color='steelblue', s=25)\n",
        "ax.set_xlabel('x')\n",
        "ax.set_ylabel('y')\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fig-fig622",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "In the following example, we generate a scatter plot using the `ax.plot` function with the option `linestyle=\"\"`. "
      ],
      "id": "721d47a3"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: fig-fig6.23\n",
        "#| fig-cap: Scatter plot using `ax.plot`\n",
        "# Scatter plot using ax.plot\n",
        "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(5, 4))\n",
        "ax.plot(x, y, linestyle='', marker='o', ms=5, color='steelblue', alpha=0.8, label=\"Data points\")\n",
        "ax.set_xlabel('x', fontsize=12)\n",
        "ax.set_ylabel('y', fontsize=12)\n",
        "# ax.set_title('Scatter plot using ax.plot', fontsize=14)\n",
        "# ax.legend()\n",
        "ax.grid(True, linestyle='--', alpha=0.5)\n",
        "plt.tight_layout()\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fig-fig623",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Bar plots\n",
        "Bar plots can be generated using the `ax.bar` function for vertical bars or the `ax.barh` function for horizontal bars. "
      ],
      "id": "1014cd34"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: fig-fig6.24\n",
        "#| fig-cap: Bar plot using `ax.bar`\n",
        "# Bar plot using ax.bar\n",
        "np.random.seed(45)\n",
        "y = np.random.rand(5)\n",
        "x = np.arange(5)\n",
        "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(5, 4))\n",
        "ax.bar(x,y, width = 1, color = 'steelblue', \n",
        "    edgecolor = '#000000', linewidth = 0.5, alpha = 0.8)\n",
        "ax.set_xlabel('x')\n",
        "ax.set_ylabel('y')\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fig-fig624",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "In the following example, we generate a horizontal bar plot using the `ax.barh` function."
      ],
      "id": "1a96a0eb"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: fig-fig6.24-1\n",
        "#| fig-cap: Horizontal bar plot using `ax.barh`\n",
        "# Horizontal Bar plot using ax.barh\n",
        "np.random.seed(45)\n",
        "y = np.random.rand(5)\n",
        "x = [\"G1\", \"G2\", \"G3\", \"G4\", \"G5\"]\n",
        "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(5, 4))\n",
        "ax.barh(x, y, height=0.5, color='steelblue', \n",
        "    edgecolor='#000000', linewidth=0.5, alpha=0.8)\n",
        "# ax.set_title('Horizontal Bar Plot')\n",
        "ax.set_xlabel('y')\n",
        "ax.set_ylabel('Groups')\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fig-fig624-1",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Pie plot\n",
        "Pie plots can be generated using the `ax.pie` function. The `explode` option can be used to separate the slices of the pie chart, and the `autopct` option can be used to display the percentage of each slice. "
      ],
      "id": "0de2a418"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: fig-fig6.25\n",
        "#| fig-cap: Pie plot\n",
        "# Pie plot\n",
        "np.random.seed(45)\n",
        "y = np.random.rand(5)\n",
        "y = y/sum(y)\n",
        "y[y<.05] = .05\n",
        "labels=['Group 1','Group 2','Group 3','Group 4','Group 5']\n",
        "colors = ['#FF0000','#FFFF00','#00FF00','#00FFFF','#0000FF']\n",
        "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(5, 5))\n",
        "ax.pie(y,labels=labels,colors=colors, \n",
        "    shadow=True, explode=np.array([0.1,0,0,0.1,0]), autopct='%1.1f%%', startangle=90)\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fig-fig625",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Histograms\n",
        "The `ax.hist` function is used to create a histogram. In the following example, the `density` option takes a boolean value that determines whether the histogram is normalized to form a probability density function (PDF). "
      ],
      "id": "4b655a0e"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: fig-fig6.26\n",
        "#| fig-cap: Histogram\n",
        "#| layout-ncol: 2\n",
        "#| fig-subcap:\n",
        "#|   - First Histogram\n",
        "#|   - Second Histogram\n",
        "# First Histogram\n",
        "np.random.seed(45)\n",
        "x = np.random.randn(1000)\n",
        "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(5, 4))\n",
        "ax.hist(x, bins=30, density=False, color='steelblue', edgecolor='black', alpha=0.8)\n",
        "ax.set_title('Histogram')\n",
        "ax.set_xlabel('x')\n",
        "ax.set_ylabel('Frequency')\n",
        "plt.tight_layout()\n",
        "plt.show()\n",
        "\n",
        "# Second Histogram\n",
        "x = np.random.randn(1000)\n",
        "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(5, 4))\n",
        "ax.hist(x, bins=30, density=True, color='#FF7F00', edgecolor='black', alpha=0.8)\n",
        "ax.set_title('Normalized Histogram')\n",
        "ax.set_xlabel('x')\n",
        "ax.set_ylabel('Density')\n",
        "plt.tight_layout()\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fig-fig626",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "In the following example, we show how to add multiple plots to the same axes. The first plot is a histogram, and the second plot is the probability density function (PDF) of the normal distribution."
      ],
      "id": "a8329690"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: fig-fig6.28\n",
        "#| fig-cap: Multiple plots on the same axes\n",
        "#| layout-ncol: 2\n",
        "#| fig-subcap:\n",
        "#|   - First Histogram\n",
        "#|   - Second Histogram\n",
        "# First Histogram with PDF overlay (OOP style, improved)\n",
        "np.random.seed(45)\n",
        "x = np.random.randn(500)\n",
        "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(5, 4))\n",
        "# Plot histogram\n",
        "ax.hist(x, bins=30, label='Empirical', density=True, color='steelblue', edgecolor='black', alpha=0.7)\n",
        "# Overlay normal PDF\n",
        "pdfx = np.linspace(x.min(), x.max(), 500)\n",
        "pdfy = stats.norm.pdf(pdfx)\n",
        "ax.plot(pdfx, pdfy, 'k-', label='Normal PDF', linewidth=1)\n",
        "# Labels and legend\n",
        "ax.set_xlabel('x')\n",
        "ax.set_ylabel('Density')\n",
        "ax.set_title('Histogram with Normal PDF')\n",
        "ax.legend()\n",
        "fig.tight_layout()\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fig-fig628",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Multiple plots on the same figure\n",
        "In the following example, we first create a `Figure` of size 8x7 inches using `fig=plt.figure(figsize=(8,7))`. We then create the 2x2 grid of subplots and the `Axes` objects with `fig.add_subplot(2,2,p)`, where p indicates the position of the subplot within the grid. "
      ],
      "id": "a150223b"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: fig-fig6.27\n",
        "#| fig-cap: Using the `fig.add_subplot` function\n",
        "np.random.seed(45)\n",
        "fig = plt.figure(figsize=(7, 7))\n",
        "\n",
        "# Panel 1: Line plot\n",
        "ax1 = fig.add_subplot(2, 2, 1)\n",
        "y1 = np.random.randn(100)\n",
        "ax1.plot(y1, color='steelblue', linewidth=1)\n",
        "ax1.set_title('Line Plot')\n",
        "ax1.set_xlabel('Index')\n",
        "ax1.set_ylabel('Value')\n",
        "ax1.grid(True, linestyle='--', alpha=0.5)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Panel 2: Bar plot\n",
        "ax2 = fig.add_subplot(2, 2, 2)\n",
        "y2 = np.random.rand(5)\n",
        "x2 = np.arange(5)\n",
        "ax2.bar(x2, y2, label='Bar Chart', color='orange', edgecolor='black', linewidth=0.7, alpha=0.8)\n",
        "ax2.legend()\n",
        "ax2.set_xlabel('x label')\n",
        "ax2.set_ylabel('y label')\n",
        "ax2.set_title('Bar Plot')\n",
        "ax2.grid(axis='y', linestyle=':', alpha=0.5)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Panel 3: Pie plot\n",
        "ax3 = fig.add_subplot(2, 2, 3)\n",
        "y3 = np.random.rand(5)\n",
        "y3 = y3 / y3.sum()\n",
        "y3[y3 < .05] = .05\n",
        "labels = ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D', 'E']\n",
        "colors = ['#FF00FF', '#FFF000', '#00FF0F', '#00FFF0', '#F0000F']\n",
        "ax3.pie(y3, labels=labels, colors=colors, autopct='%1.1f%%', startangle=90)\n",
        "ax3.set_title('Pie Plot')\n",
        "\n",
        "# Panel 4: Scatter plot\n",
        "ax4 = fig.add_subplot(2, 2, 4)\n",
        "z = np.random.randn(100, 2)\n",
        "z[:, 1] = 0.5 * z[:, 0] + np.sqrt(0.5) * z[:, 1]\n",
        "x4 = z[:, 0]\n",
        "y4 = z[:, 1]\n",
        "ax4.scatter(x4, y4, s=25, color='steelblue', alpha=0.8, edgecolor='black')\n",
        "ax4.set_title('Scatter Plot')\n",
        "ax4.set_xlabel('x values')\n",
        "ax4.set_ylabel('y values')\n",
        "ax4.grid(True, linestyle='--', alpha=0.5)\n",
        "\n",
        "fig.subplots_adjust(hspace=0.4, wspace=0.3)\n",
        "fig.tight_layout()\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fig-fig627",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Alternatively, we can create the 2x2 grid of subplots and  the `Axes` objects using `fig, ax = plt.subplots(2,2,figsize=(8,7))`. The positions of subplots are specified through `ax[i,j]`."
      ],
      "id": "773861f2"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: fig-fig6.27-f1\n",
        "#| fig-cap: Using the `plt.subplots` function\n",
        "np.random.seed(45)\n",
        "fig, ax = plt.subplots(2, 2, figsize=(7, 7))\n",
        "\n",
        "# Panel 1: Line plot\n",
        "y1 = np.random.randn(100)\n",
        "ax[0, 0].plot(y1, color='steelblue', linewidth=1)\n",
        "ax[0, 0].set_title('Line Plot')\n",
        "ax[0, 0].set_xlabel('Index')\n",
        "ax[0, 0].set_ylabel('Value')\n",
        "ax[0, 0].grid(True, linestyle='--', alpha=0.5)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Panel 2: Bar plot\n",
        "y2 = np.random.rand(5)\n",
        "x2 = np.arange(5)\n",
        "ax[0, 1].bar(x2, y2, label='Bar Plot', color='orange', edgecolor='black', linewidth=0.7, alpha=0.8)\n",
        "ax[0, 1].legend()\n",
        "ax[0, 1].set_xlabel('x label')\n",
        "ax[0, 1].set_ylabel('y label')\n",
        "ax[0, 1].set_title('Bar Plot')\n",
        "ax[0, 1].grid(axis='y', linestyle=':', alpha=0.5)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Panel 3: Pie plot\n",
        "y3 = np.random.rand(5)\n",
        "y3 = y3 / y3.sum()\n",
        "y3[y3 < 0.05] = 0.05\n",
        "labels = ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D', 'E']\n",
        "colors = ['#FF00FF', '#FFF000', '#00FF0F', '#00FFF0', '#F0000F']\n",
        "ax[1, 0].pie(y3, labels=labels, colors=colors, autopct='%1.1f%%', startangle=90)\n",
        "ax[1, 0].set_title('Pie Plot')\n",
        "\n",
        "# Panel 4: Scatter plot\n",
        "z = np.random.randn(100, 2)\n",
        "z[:, 1] = 0.5 * z[:, 0] + np.sqrt(0.5) * z[:, 1]\n",
        "x4 = z[:, 0]\n",
        "y4 = z[:, 1]\n",
        "ax[1, 1].scatter(x4, y4, s=25, color='steelblue', alpha=0.8, edgecolor='black')\n",
        "ax[1, 1].set_title('Scatter Plot')\n",
        "ax[1, 1].set_xlabel('x values')\n",
        "ax[1, 1].set_ylabel('y values')\n",
        "ax[1, 1].grid(True, linestyle='--', alpha=0.5)\n",
        "\n",
        "fig.subplots_adjust(hspace=0.4, wspace=0.3)\n",
        "fig.tight_layout()\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fig-fig627-f1",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Other Tools for Graphics\n",
        "In this chapter, we covered only the most commonly used plotting methods. Matplotlib also provides functions for other types of plots, such as density plots, contour plots, and three-dimensional plots, as well as customization tools for legends, colorbars, ticks, text, and annotations. In addition to Matplotlib, the Seaborn and Pandas modules offer additional plotting methods. For further reading, we refer the reader to @Sheppard2021, @Wes2022, and @Jake2023.  "
      ],
      "id": "fdd8635b"
    }
